# Knuth Style Solver As a Stronger Baseline

After building and testing the heuristic agents, I started looking at the problem differently.

My first approaches were based mostly on my own intuition about how the puzzle should be solved.

I focused on remembering previous guesses, checking whether new candidates were consistent with the feedback.

Using that knowledge to make local changes to promising guesses, and eventually combining these ideas with some information based selection.

That was useful, but it also meant I was mostly asking:

> “How can I make my current strategy better?”

I wanted to step back and ask a different question:

> “What does a strong solution to this type of problem actually look like?”

## Looking Beyond My Own Heuristics

This led me to Mastermind and eventually to Knuth's approach to solving it.

The permutation puzzle I built is essentially a black peg style Mastermind problem:

the hidden answer is a permutation, guesses are permutations, and the only feedback is the number of positions that are correct.

That gave me something I didn't have before: a more principled reference for what can be achieved without reinforcement learning.

Instead of continuing to patch my existing heuristics, I decided to implement a Knuth style solver and use it as another benchmark.

## A Different Way of Thinking About the Puzzle

My original agents were largely built around ideas I developed while experimenting with the environment.

The Systematic Agent remembered previous guesses and used consistency checks:

* Keep the history of guesses and scores.
* Generate a candidate.
* Check it against all previous feedback.
* Keep it only if it remains consistent.

The Logic Agent went further by staying around the best known guess and exploring local changes.

The Hybrid Agent tried to combine these ideas with candidate selection based on information diversity.

These approaches were useful because they came from actually interacting with the problem.

But they also reflected my limited understanding of the underlying search problem.

The Knuth style approach gave me a different perspective.

Rather than mainly asking whether a candidate is consistent with what I already know,

it considers how a guess can divide the remaining possibilities based on the feedback it could produce.

That changes the purpose of a guess.

It isn't only about finding a candidate that could be the answer.

It can also be about choosing a guess that tells us the most useful information about the remaining search space.

That distinction was important for me.

## The Benchmark Changed

Once I implemented the solver, I added it to the same simulation and benchmarking setup used for the other agents.

At the moment, the Knuth style solver is sitting at the top of the benchmark.

This isn't based on a single lucky run.

Because the puzzle can be simulated repeatedly, I can run multiple episodes and observe how the different strategies behave across the same kind of environment.

That makes the benchmark much more useful than simply seeing one agent solve the puzzle.

I can actually watch the difference between strategies emerge:

* how quickly they reduce the search space,
* how consistently they solve,
* how their behavior changes as the puzzle gets larger,
* and where the weaker heuristics start struggling.

The important result for me isn't simply that the Knuth style solver is currently the strongest.

It's that I now have a much better reference point for the RL aspect.

## What This Taught Me About My Earlier Approaches

Looking back, my consistency based approaches were not wrong.

They were just solving a narrower problem.

Checking whether a candidate is consistent with every previous observation is useful because it eliminates impossible states.

But once a large number of possibilities remain, simply knowing what is possible isn't enough.

The next question becomes:

Which action should I take to learn the most from the next observation?

That is where the information gain perspective becomes much more important.

This also helped me understand why some of my earlier ideas became increasingly complicated as the puzzle scaled.

I was trying to improve the behavior of the search without fully understanding the structure of the information available to the solver.

Finding a stronger algorithm gave me a way to separate those two things.

I can now compare my own ideas against a more principled strategy instead of assuming that more heuristics automatically means better search.

## The Bigger Lesson

This was probably the most useful part of implementing the Knuth style solver.

I started this project knowing some reinforcement learning theory and having some intuition about search and games.

Then, while trying to make the puzzle work, I ended up learning about:

* constraint satisfaction,
* search space representation,
* global consistency,
* information gain,
* Mastermind strategies,
* minimax style candidate selection,
* and the relationship between observations and future decisions.

Some of these topics weren't things I originally planned to study.

They appeared because the problem forced me to look for them.

That is becoming a recurring pattern in this playground.

I don't necessarily start with the complete set of knowledge needed to solve a problem.

I start with what I understand, build something, find where my understanding breaks down, and then learn whatever is necessary to investigate that gap.

Sometimes that means going into a topic that doesn't seem directly related to RL at first.

But those detours can change how I understand the original problem.

## Why This Matters for the RL Version

This also changes how I want to approach the reinforcement learning side of the puzzle.

I don't want the RL agent to simply be another complicated heuristic and then compare it against poorly understood baselines.

The Knuth style solver gives me something concrete to learn from.

I can study what information the solver uses, what makes its decisions effective, and which parts of that reasoning are explicit in the algorithm.

Then I can return to the RL environment and ask a much better question:

> Can an agent learn useful decision making behavior from the same observations, rather than having the search strategy explicitly programmed into it?

That is much closer to what I actually want to investigate.

The goal isn't necessarily to immediately beat the Knuth style solver.

The goal is to understand what the RL formulation is learning, what it can discover on its own, and what information it actually needs to make good decisions.

## Next

With the heuristic side now much better understood and a strong baseline established, the next stage is to properly work through the RL experiment.

That means going back to the environment, observation design, action space, reward, training setup, and evaluation, and making sure I actually understand what I am asking the agent to learn.

For me, this is where the project becomes much more interesting.

I started with a small thought from a game I enjoyed playing with friends.

I built the first strategies I could think of.

They failed in unexpected ways.

I learned why.

Then I found algorithms and ideas I didn't know I needed.

Now I have a stronger solver sitting above my original approaches, and a much clearer idea of what I want the RL agent to try to learn.
